{"id":"decision-example-001","type":"decision","fact":"Example: Chose Zustand over Redux for client state management because of simpler API and smaller bundle size.","recommendation":"Use Zustand stores for client state. Use TanStack Query for server state. Do not mix the two patterns.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"human","reference":"Architecture decision","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["architecture","state-management","zustand"],"affectedFiles":["src/stores/**/*.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-posix-pty-firewall-001","type":"decision","fact":"Only PureClaw.Backend.Pty and PureClaw.Backend.Pty.Fake may import System.Posix.Pty (or the posix-pty package). The firewall is enforced by scripts/check-pty-firewall.sh, wired into .githooks/pre-push and .git/hooks/pre-push. This keeps the upstream-dormant posix-pty dependency contained to two modules so it can be replaced as a single unit.","recommendation":"Do NOT add 'posix-pty' or 'System.Posix.Pty' imports outside src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty.hs and src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty/Fake.hs. Any factory that needs PTY I/O takes a PtyIO record as a parameter and delegates. The pre-push hook will block any violation.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"feat/terminal-backends-design","reference":"WU7 PtyIO seam + CI gate","date":"2026-05-14"}],"tags":["architecture","dependency-firewall","backend","security"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty.hs","src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty/Fake.hs","scripts/check-pty-firewall.sh",".githooks/pre-push"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-tmux-detach-only-typed-001","type":"decision","fact":"A tmux Attach-mode BackendHandle's close path is type-restricted to detach-only via 'data TmuxCloseAction = TmuxDetach' — a one-constructor sum with the constructor NOT exported. There is no API surface that allows a caller to ask for kill-window / kill-session / kill-pane. Close writes only the Ctrl-B + 'd' key sequence into the existing PTY.","recommendation":"When a destructive operation must be impossible-by-API, encode the permitted action set as a closed sum type with the constructors NOT exported. The close path takes a value of that type; downstream code can only construct the values exported by the module. This is stronger than 'don't call kill-window' as a code-review rule.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"feat/terminal-backends-design","reference":"WU10 polish commit","date":"2026-05-14"}],"tags":["security","type-safety","tmux","api-design"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Backend/Tmux.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-allowlist-loud-default-001","type":"decision","fact":"Remote channels (Signal, Telegram) resolve missing/empty allow_from AND dm_policy=\"open\" to AllowAll. A channel resolving to AllowAll must emit a prominent startup warning (multi-line banner to stdout + WARN to stderr), never silently accept everyone. The warning is wired at the activation boundary: Signal in runChat's \"signal\" branch before the signal-cli check (so it fires even on CLI fallback); Telegram in withTelegramChannel.","recommendation":"When adding a channel, resolve its allow-list and call warnIfOpenAllowList at the activation point. Never let an open allow-list be silent.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"PR #73","date":"2026-05-31"}],"tags":["security","channels","allow-list"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/CLI/Commands.hs","src/PureClaw/Channels/Telegram.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-telegram-allowlist-dormant-001","type":"decision","fact":"Telegram has no live boot path (webhook-driven; gateway out of scope), so withTelegramChannel and resolveTelegramConfig are fully unit-tested but production-dormant — no production caller until the gateway boot path is built. The effectiveChannel switch in runChat still handles only \"signal\"/\"cli\". Accepted YAGNI tradeoff: the seams mirror the Signal equivalents so the gateway inherits enforcement+warning with no rework.","recommendation":"When wiring the Telegram gateway boot path, route through withTelegramChannel so the allow-list warning and enforcement activate automatically.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"PR #73","date":"2026-05-31"}],"tags":["channels","telegram","architecture"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Channels/Telegram.hs","src/PureClaw/CLI/Commands.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
